## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design end-to-end prompt orchestration under SLA; covers multi-model choice, caching, and budget-based pruning in a multi-tenant context.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic plan generation with per-model latency and cost profiles
- Content-addressable caching to reduce repeated work
- Budget-aware pruning and graceful degradation
- Tenant isolation and guardrails in orchestration

## Code Example

```javascript
class MockModel {
  constructor(name, tokenCost, latencyMs){
    this.name = name; this.tokenCost = tokenCost; this.latencyMs = latencyMs;
  }
  run(prompt){
    return new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(() => resolve(`${this.name}-out:${prompt.slice(0,60)}`), this.latencyMs));
  }
}

const modelA = new MockModel('ModelA', 0.5, 120);
const modelB = new MockModel('ModelB', 0.2, 60);
const cache = new Map();
function hash(s){ let h=0; for(let i=0;i<s.length;i++){ h=(h<<5)-h+s.charCodeAt(i); h|=0;} return h.toString(); }
function plan(intent){ return [ [modelA, intent+" [segment1]"], [modelB, intent+" [segment2]"] ]; }
async function runPlan(intent, budgetTokens, maxLatencyMs=300){
  const key = hash(intent);
  if(cache.has(key)) return cache.get(key);
  const steps = plan(intent);
  let totalLatency = 0; const outputs = [];
  for(const [m,p] of steps){ const out = await m.run(p); outputs.push(out); totalLatency += m.latencyMs; if(totalLatency>maxLatencyMs) break; }
  const result = outputs.join(' | ');
  cache.set(key, result);
  return result;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
The question probes practical prompt governance: how to prove provenance and detect tampering from model transformations in a multi-tenant setting. It tests attack awareness, deterministic auditing, and safe-guard design under real-world noise.

## Key Concepts
- Watermarking robustness under paraphrase/translation
- Non-semantic embedding and tamper detection
- Audit logging for per-tenant prompts
- Edge-case handling and false positives

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal watermark prototype (JS)
function addWatermark(prompt, tag) {
  return prompt + " [WM:" + tag + "]";
}
function detectWatermark(text) {
  const m = text.match(/\[WM:([A-Za-z0-9._-]+)\]/);
  return m ? m[1] : null;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you evaluate watermark robustness across languages/models?
- How would you scale to thousands of tenants with dynamic watermark policies?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a configurable policy framework that adapts prompts based on language and content risk, with traceable moderation reasoning and backpressure.

## Key Concepts
- Language detection and content classification
- Policy routing matrix (per-language, per-category)
- Guardrails and re-prompt loops
- Audit logging for moderation workflows

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype: routePrompt(prompt, lang, category) -> {policy, rePrompt}
function routePrompt(prompt, lang, category){
  const policyKey = (category in policyMap && lang in policyMap[category]) ? policyMap[category][lang] : 'default';
  let rePrompt = null;
  const risk = scoreRisk(prompt, category, lang);
  if(risk > threshold){
    rePrompt = `Please comply with ${policyKey} before proceeding.`;
  }
  return {policy: policyKey, rePrompt};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test cross-language policy consistency?
- How would you store and audit re-prompt decisions at scale?
